-- Q9 [Thyroid & Parathyroid] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds, figure_svg)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Thyroid & Parathyroid', 'A routine metabolic panel shows that a patient''s plasma Ca²⁺ concentration has dropped below the normal range. In response, an endocrine gland releases a peptide hormone that acts on the kidney tubules to help restore calcium levels. Which effect does this hormone most likely produce at the kidney?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Reduced synthesis of active vitamin D"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Increased urinary excretion of calcium"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Increased reabsorption of filtered calcium"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Direct inhibition of renal osteoclasts"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.'' The answer is C because a fall in plasma Ca²⁺ triggers the parathyroid glands to secrete parathyroid hormone (PTH), which acts on the kidney tubules to increase reabsorption of calcium from the filtrate, thereby decreasing urinary calcium loss and returning blood calcium toward normal. (Choice C) is correct: PTH stimulates the renal tubules to reclaim filtered calcium, one of its three calcium-raising actions alongside stimulating osteoclastic bone resorption and initiating calcitriol production for intestinal absorption. (Choice B) is incorrect: increased urinary excretion of calcium is a calcitonin effect, secreted when calcium is high; it would further lower calcium and worsen the deficit, making it the reverse of the needed correction. (Choice A) is incorrect: the calcium-raising hormone initiates, not reduces, calcitriol (active vitamin D3) synthesis in the kidney, which boosts intestinal calcium absorption; reduced synthesis would lower calcium. (Choice D) is incorrect: osteoclasts reside in bone, not the kidney, and the calcium-raising hormone stimulates rather than inhibits osteoclast activity to release skeletal calcium. Reasoning from the low-calcium perturbation to the hormone''s corrective renal action requires applying the direction of calcium regulation rather than recalling a definition (Skill 2).', 'medium', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 2, 75, NULL)
  RETURNING id
)
