-- Q10 [Thyroid & Parathyroid] medium skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds, figure_svg)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Thyroid & Parathyroid', 'A researcher administers a compound that locks vitamin D in the same activated form that parathyroid hormone causes the kidney to generate. Compared with untreated controls, how would intestinal epithelial handling of dietary calcium most likely change in the treated animals?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Greater sodium uptake, as with aldosterone"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Reduced calcium uptake, mimicking calcitonin"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Unchanged calcium uptake but increased phosphate loss"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Greater calcium uptake across the gut lining"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.'' The answer is D because the activated form of vitamin D described in the stem is calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D), the metabolite that intestinal epithelial cells require in order to absorb dietary calcium; supplying more of this active form therefore drives greater calcium uptake across the gut lining. (Choice D) is correct: calcitriol stimulates increased absorption of dietary calcium by the intestines, so a compound that maintains vitamin D in this active state enhances, rather than diminishes, calcium uptake across the intestinal epithelium. (Choice B) is incorrect: it confuses the effect of active vitamin D with that of calcitonin, the thyroid parafollicular-cell hormone that opposes calcium accumulation and reduces absorption of dietary calcium; active vitamin D promotes absorption rather than suppressing it. (Choice C) is incorrect: increased renal phosphate loss is a direct effect of parathyroid hormone on the kidney, not a consequence of activating vitamin D, which acts on the intestine to raise calcium absorption rather than leaving it unchanged. (Choice A) is incorrect: sodium reabsorption is governed by aldosterone from the adrenal cortex and is unrelated to the vitamin D pathway, which selectively enhances calcium handling rather than sodium handling. Skill 2 is demonstrated by tracing a perturbation of the vitamin D activation pathway to its predicted downstream physiological outcome at the intestinal epithelium rather than recalling a definition.', 'medium', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 2, 80, NULL)
  RETURNING id
)
