-- Q11 [Thyroid & Parathyroid] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds, figure_svg)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Thyroid & Parathyroid', 'During a thyroidectomy, a surgeon inadvertently excises all four small glands embedded on the posterior thyroid surface, and the patient''s serum shows the change days later. What is the most likely direction of the patient''s blood calcium, and why?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Falls; three calcium-raising pathways are lost"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Rises; unopposed calcitonin frees skeletal calcium"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Falls; aldosterone secretion is now suppressed"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Rises; renal calcium excretion is blocked"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.'' The answer is A because the excised glands are the parathyroid glands, whose hormone (PTH) is the principal defender of blood calcium: it stimulates osteoclast-mediated bone resorption, promotes calcium reabsorption from the renal tubules, and initiates renal synthesis of calcitriol (active vitamin D), which drives intestinal calcium absorption. Removing the glands eliminates all three calcium-raising actions at once, so serum calcium falls (hypocalcemia). (Choice A) is correct: with PTH gone, bone release, renal reabsorption, and vitamin-D-mediated gut uptake of calcium all cease, and the net result is a drop in blood calcium. (Choice B) is incorrect: calcitonin (from the thyroid''s parafollicular C cells) lowers blood calcium; even if calcitonin were left unopposed, it would push calcium down, not up, and its physiologic role in humans is minor compared with PTH. (Choice C) is incorrect: aldosterone is an adrenal mineralocorticoid governing sodium and potassium, not calcium, and its secretion is unaffected by parathyroid removal, so it is the wrong hormonal axis. (Choice D) is incorrect: loss of PTH reduces renal calcium reabsorption, meaning more calcium is lost in the urine rather than less, which would lower calcium and cannot explain a rise. Answering requires chaining the identity of the removed glands to the coordinated loss of three PTH-dependent pathways and predicting the net effect on serum calcium, a Skill 2 multi-step application of endocrine physiology.', 'hard', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 2, 100, NULL)
  RETURNING id
)
