-- Q12 [Thyroid & Parathyroid] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds, figure_svg)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Thyroid & Parathyroid', 'A patient''s serum calcium reading falls to 7.8 mg/dL (normal 8.5–10.2 mg/dL) after several weeks of dietary calcium deficiency. Homeostatic feedback triggers secretion of the hormone that will drive this value back toward the normal range. Which hormone is secreted?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Calcitonin"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Parathyroid hormone"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Calcitriol (active vitamin D)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Aldosterone"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.'' The answer is B because the patient''s serum calcium has dropped below the normal range, and the correction required is an increase in blood calcium; parathyroid hormone (PTH) is the hormone secreted in response to low blood calcium, and it raises the concentration by stimulating osteoclasts to resorb bone, promoting renal calcium reabsorption, and indirectly increasing intestinal calcium absorption. (Choice B) is correct: PTH, secreted by the chief cells of the parathyroid glands in response to falling blood calcium, elevates serum calcium and is therefore the hormone that would move a low value back toward normal. (Choice A) is incorrect: calcitonin, released by the parafollicular (C) cells of the thyroid, is secreted in response to elevated blood calcium and lowers it, which is the opposite of the correction this hypocalcemic patient requires. (Choice C) is incorrect: calcitriol does raise blood calcium by increasing intestinal absorption, but its formation is a downstream step triggered by PTH rather than the primary hormone secreted directly in response to the low-calcium signal; the discriminating hormone the feedback loop secretes is PTH. (Choice D) is incorrect: aldosterone is a mineralocorticoid that regulates sodium retention, potassium excretion, and blood volume, and it does not govern the blood calcium set point. Answering requires applying the concept that a hormone secreted to correct hypocalcemia must be one that raises calcium, then selecting the source hormone whose secretion is triggered directly by the low-calcium signal.', 'medium', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 2, 75, NULL)
  RETURNING id
)
