-- Q18 [Adrenal Glands] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds, figure_svg)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Adrenal Glands', 'A hypertensive patient begins a drug that inhibits a lung-localized enzyme responsible for cleaving a circulating peptide into its vasoactive form. After several days on the drug, which change in adrenal cortex output and blood pressure would be expected?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Reduced cortisol; lowered blood pressure"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Reduced aldosterone; lowered blood pressure"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Elevated aldosterone; raised blood pressure"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Reduced epinephrine; lowered blood pressure"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.'' The answer is B because the described drug is an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor: ACE, located in the lungs, converts inactive angiotensin I into active angiotensin II. Angiotensin II is the direct stimulus that signals the adrenal cortex (zona glomerulosa) to secrete aldosterone. Blocking ACE therefore lowers angiotensin II, so aldosterone secretion falls; because aldosterone normally drives Na+ (and thus water) reabsorption and angiotensin II normally drives vasoconstriction, losing both effects reduces blood volume and vascular tone, lowering blood pressure. (Choice B) is correct: less angiotensin II removes the stimulus for aldosterone, and the combined loss of Na+/water retention plus vasoconstriction lowers blood pressure, which is precisely why ACE inhibitors treat hypertension. (Choice A) is incorrect: cortisol is a glucocorticoid from the zona fasciculata whose secretion is governed by the hypothalamic CRH to anterior-pituitary ACTH axis, not by angiotensin II; blocking ACE does not remove cortisol''s stimulus, so cortisol output is not the hormone that changes here, even though it also originates in the adrenal cortex. (Choice C) is incorrect: this reverses the causal chain. Blocking the enzyme reduces angiotensin II, so aldosterone falls rather than rises, and blood pressure decreases; a rise in both would occur only if angiotensin II production increased. (Choice D) is incorrect: epinephrine is a catecholamine released by the adrenal medulla, not a hormone of the adrenal cortex, and its release is controlled by sympathetic (preganglionic) input rather than the angiotensin pathway, so it is unaffected by ACE inhibition. This question requires Skill 2 reasoning because the student must trace a multi-step signaling chain from a blocked enzyme through the missing peptide to the downstream hormone and then predict the integrated physiological outcome, rather than recalling a single stated fact.', 'hard', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 2, 105, NULL)
  RETURNING id
)
