  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Adrenal Glands', 'In a post-menopausal patient whose ovaries have ceased functioning, an adrenal steroid secreted by the deepest region of the cortex becomes the principal precursor that peripheral tissues convert into estrogens, and it neither raises blood glucose nor promotes sodium retention. This steroid is best classified as which of the following?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A glucocorticoid"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A mineralocorticoid"}, {"label": "C", "text": "An adrenal androgen"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A medullary catecholamine"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.'' The answer is C because the deepest cortical region, the zona reticularis, secretes a class of steroid sex hormones called adrenal androgens; these supplement gonadal androgens and are converted in peripheral tissues to testosterone or estrogens, which is why they become the main source of estrogens after the ovaries decline. (Choice C) is correct: adrenal androgens are the cortical sex hormones produced by the zona reticularis, and because they serve as the estrogen precursor in a post-menopausal patient and act on neither glucose nor salt balance, this classification matches every feature described. (Choice B) is incorrect: mineralocorticoids such as aldosterone come from the zona glomerulosa and regulate sodium and potassium balance, but the stem specifies the steroid does not promote sodium retention, ruling out this class. (Choice A) is incorrect: glucocorticoids such as cortisol come from the zona fasciculata and raise blood glucose, yet the stem states the steroid does not raise blood glucose, so it cannot belong to this class. (Choice D) is incorrect: catecholamines such as epinephrine originate in the adrenal medulla and are amino-acid-derived, not steroids of the cortex, so a medullary catecholamine cannot be the cortical precursor described. Reaching the answer requires applying knowledge of which cortical zone produces which hormone class to a steroid defined only by its function, rather than recalling a stated definition.', 'medium', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 2, 75, NULL)
