WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds, figure_svg)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Adrenal Glands', 'During an acute fright, sympathetic input triggers the chromaffin cells at the core of an adrenal gland to dump a fast-acting signal into the bloodstream within seconds. Which hormones are released, and to which chemical class do they belong?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Insulin and glucagon; proteins"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Cortisol and aldosterone; steroids"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Thyroxine and triiodothyronine; amines"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Epinephrine and norepinephrine; amines"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.'' The answer is D because the chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla—the inner core of the adrenal gland driven by the sympathetic nervous system during acute stress—secrete epinephrine and norepinephrine directly into the systemic circulation, and because these two hormones are synthesized from the amino acid tyrosine, they are chemically classified as catecholamines, a type of amine hormone. (Choice D) is correct: the adrenal medulla''s chromaffin cells release epinephrine and norepinephrine as the rapid fight-or-flight signal, and Table 17.5 lists both under the chemical class "Amine." (Choice B) is incorrect: cortisol and aldosterone are steroids, but they are produced by the adrenal cortex (the outer region), and cortisol is the slow-acting long-term stress hormone, not the seconds-fast medullary signal. (Choice C) is incorrect: thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3) are indeed amine-class hormones, but they are secreted by the thyroid gland to set basal metabolic rate over hours to days, not by the adrenal medulla during acute fright. (Choice A) is incorrect: insulin and glucagon are protein/peptide hormones from the pancreatic islets that regulate blood glucose, not catecholamines released by the adrenal medulla. As a Skill 1 item, this question asks the student to recall the specific hormones secreted by the adrenal medulla and their chemical classification.', 'easy', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 1, 45, NULL)
  RETURNING id
