-- Q22 [Adrenal Glands] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds, figure_svg)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Endocrine System', 'Adrenal Glands', 'Two hormones are extracted from a single adrenal gland. One is synthesized from cholesterol and exerts effects that build over hours; the other is synthesized from the amino acid tyrosine and takes effect within seconds. From which parts of the gland do these two hormones originate, in that order?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Both from the cortex"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The medulla, then the cortex"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The cortex, then the medulla"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Both from the medulla"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.''

The answer is C because the adrenal cortex builds steroid hormones from cholesterol, and because these act by changing gene transcription their effects develop slowly over hours; the adrenal medulla builds catecholamines from the amino acid tyrosine, and because these act through rapid second-messenger signaling their effects appear within seconds. The cholesterol-derived, slow hormone is thus cortical and the tyrosine-derived, fast hormone is medullary.

(Choice C) is correct: the cholesterol-derived, slow-acting hormone comes from the cortex, and the tyrosine-derived, fast-acting hormone comes from the medulla.

(Choice B) is incorrect: this reverses the two regions; the cortex (not the medulla) makes the cholesterol-based steroid, and the medulla (not the cortex) makes the tyrosine-based catecholamine.

(Choice A) is incorrect: the cortex makes only the steroid; the fast, tyrosine-derived catecholamine is a medullary product, so both cannot originate in the cortex.

(Choice D) is incorrect: the medulla makes only the catecholamine; the slow, cholesterol-derived steroid is a cortical product, so both cannot originate in the medulla.

This Skill 2 item asks the student to infer each hormone''s source region from its chemical precursor and time course rather than from a stated label.', 'medium', '3A', 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources', 'biology', 2, 75, NULL)
  RETURNING id
)
